{
    "esm": "1.0.0",
    "metadata": {
        "name": "COOxidation",
        "description": "CO oxidation chemistry diagnostics from Seinfeld & Pandis (2006) Ch. 6 §6.3 Eqs. 6.9-6.18, pp. 212-219. CO oxidation is the simplest hydrocarbon-oxidation mechanism and illustrates the catalytic HOx cycling that drives tropospheric ozone production (net: CO + 2O₂ + hν → CO₂ + O₃, with OH regenerated through HO₂ + NO). This observed-only algebraic component implements six diagnostic relations: the OH loss rate L_OH (to CO, NO₂, O₃), the HO₂ loss rate L_HO2 (to NO, the HO₂ self-reaction, and O₃), the HOx radical-termination loss rate L_HOx (OH+NO₂→HNO₃ plus the HO₂ self-reaction), the high-NOx HO₂ steady-state concentration HO2_ss (Eq. 6.18), the net ozone production rate P_O3 (HO₂+NO production minus the OH+O₃ and HO₂+O₃ loss channels), and the HOx chain length (propagation rate over termination rate — the number of OH-HO₂ cycles before radical termination). Observed-only algebraic system (no time derivatives): twelve variables (six inputs CO/OH/HO₂/NO/NO₂/O₃ encoded as parameters, six outputs L_OH/L_HO2/L_HOx/HO2_ss/P_O3/chain_length as state), six rate-constant parameters (k_CO_OH, k_HO2_NO, k_HO2_HO2, k_OH_NO2, k_HO2_O3, k_OH_O3), one dimensionless stoichiometric constant (two), and six algebraic equations. Migrated from GasChem.jl@8c12c048 src/co_oxidation.jl:88 (`COOxidation` @component).",
        "tags": [
            "gaschem",
            "co-oxidation",
            "hox-cycling",
            "ozone-production",
            "tropospheric-chemistry",
            "observed-only",
            "seinfeld-pandis"
        ],
        "created": "2026-05-17T00:00:00Z",
        "authors": [
            "GasChem.jl contributors",
            "EarthSciModels migration (esm-kzix)"
        ],
        "references": [
            {
                "citation": "Seinfeld, J. H., & Pandis, S. N. (2006). Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics: From Air Pollution to Climate Change, 2nd ed. Wiley. Chapter 6 §6.3, Eqs. 6.9-6.18, pp. 212-219. CO oxidation as the simplest hydrocarbon-oxidation mechanism: the HOx catalytic cycle (CO + OH → CO₂ + H; H + O₂ + M → HO₂ + M; HO₂ + NO → OH + NO₂; NO₂ + hν → NO + O; O + O₂ + M → O₃ + M), the HO₂ steady state, the HOx chain length, and net ozone production. Rate constants from Table B.1."
            }
        ]
    },
    "models": {
        "COOxidation": {
            "reference": {
                "notes": "source_ref: GasChem.jl@8c12c048482b515fb8eb2110bf8ab4b4f4e71309:src/co_oxidation.jl:88\n\nUpstream `COOxidation` is an MTK `@component` with 12 `@variables` (6 inputs CO/OH/HO₂/NO/NO₂/O₃ + 6 outputs P_O3/L_HOx/L_OH/L_HO2/chain_length/HO2_ss), 6 `@parameters` (k_CO_OH, k_HO2_NO, k_HO2_HO2, k_OH_NO2, k_HO2_O3, k_OH_O3), 1 `@constants` (two), and 6 algebraic equations from S&P Ch. 6 §6.3 Eqs. 6.9-6.18. The upstream test compiles the system with `compile_with_inputs(sys, [:CO, :OH, :HO2, :NO, :NO2, :O3])` and solves it as a `NonlinearProblem` with the six inputs pinned to user-supplied concentrations.\n\nVariable-classification convention: the six input @variables (CO, OH, HO₂, NO, NO₂, O₃) are encoded here as `type: parameter` because they have no defining equation within the COOxidation component itself — they are externally-supplied inputs that the upstream compile treats via `compile_with_inputs(...)` (i.e. `mtkcompile` with `inputs=[...]`). This mirrors the rig-wide convention applied by sibling ope.esm (its five input @variables OH/HO₂/RO₂/NO/NO₂ are encoded as `type: parameter`) and oh_production.esm/stefan_boltzmann.esm/typical.esm. The six output @variables (L_OH, L_HO2, L_HOx, HO2_ss, P_O3, chain_length) are encoded as `type: state` because each has exactly one defining algebraic equation. The single upstream `@constants` entry `two` (the stoichiometric factor 2 for the HO₂ self-reaction HO₂ + HO₂ → H₂O₂ + O₂, which consumes two HO₂ radicals per event) is preserved verbatim as `type: parameter` with a fixed default of 2.0 — mirroring the constant→parameter convention of sibling oh_production.esm (`two`), chapman.esm, gwp.esm and stefan_boltzmann.esm: encoding it as a named dimensionless parameter lets MTK's unit checker treat it as a named quantity rather than a magic literal in the L_HO2 and L_HOx right-hand sides, and lets downstream consumers override the stoichiometric factor for sensitivity studies. The upstream structural test `COOxidation: Structural Verification` (test/test_co_oxidation.jl:4-32) asserts `length(unknowns(sys)) == 12`, `length(equations(sys)) == 6`, and `length(parameters(sys)) == 7` — the .esm preserves this with the 12 declared @variable entries (6 type:parameter inputs + 6 type:state outputs), 6 entries in `equations`, and 7 declared parameter entries (the 6 rate constants + the `two` constant; MTK counts `@constants` in `parameters(sys)`, hence 7, not 6).\n\nRate-constant CGS→SI translation: the upstream Julia declares the six rate constants as the SI-converted CGS values via explicit multiplication by 1e-6 (the cm³/molec/s → m³/s factor for bimolecular rate coefficients):\n  k_CO_OH   = 2.4e-13 · 1.0e-6 = 2.4e-19 m³/s   (CO + OH → CO₂ + H, S&P §6.3 R6.9 — the chain-initiating CO-consumption step)\n  k_HO2_NO  = 8.1e-12 · 1.0e-6 = 8.1e-18 m³/s   (HO₂ + NO → OH + NO₂, R6.11 — the O₃-producing chain-propagation step that regenerates OH)\n  k_HO2_HO2 = 2.9e-12 · 1.0e-6 = 2.9e-18 m³/s   (HO₂ + HO₂ → H₂O₂ + O₂, R6.15 — the low-NOx HOx self-termination)\n  k_OH_NO2  = 1.0e-11 · 1.0e-6 = 1.0e-17 m³/s   (OH + NO₂ + M → HNO₃, R6.12 — the high-NOx HOx/NOx termination)\n  k_HO2_O3  = 2.0e-15 · 1.0e-6 = 2.0e-21 m³/s   (HO₂ + O₃ → OH + 2O₂ — an O₃-loss channel)\n  k_OH_O3   = 7.3e-14 · 1.0e-6 = 7.3e-20 m³/s   (OH + O₃ → HO₂ + O₂ — an O₃-loss channel)\nThe .esm encodes the clean SI literals 2.4e-19, 8.1e-18, 2.9e-18, 1.0e-17, 2.0e-21, 7.3e-20 directly — matching the SI-literal convention of sibling ope.esm and oh_production.esm. Three of the six literals are bit-identical to the upstream Julia product (k_HO2_NO 8.1e-18, k_HO2_HO2 2.9e-18, k_OH_O3 7.3e-20); the other three differ from the product by exactly one ULP (k_CO_OH 2.4e-19 vs the product 2.3999999999999997e-19, k_OH_NO2 1.0e-17 vs 9.999999999999999e-18, k_HO2_O3 2.0e-21 vs 2.0000000000000002e-21 — each a ~1e-16 relative difference). Every inline-test expected value below is computed from the .esm's own declared literal defaults, so the file is internally exact at the rel=1e-10 inline-test tolerance regardless of that choice; the 1-ULP literal-vs-product difference is ~6 orders of magnitude below rel=1e-10 and far below the upstream tests' own rtol=0.01-0.05 acceptance bounds.\n\nObservability classification: this component is purely algebraic — every output variable is the LHS of one algebraic equation in the inputs, parameters, and (for chain_length) another output. No equation contains a `D(·)` operator, so the `(t)` time index has no role and the trajectory is constant in time. Test and example scenarios use `time_span: [0.0, 1.0]` and sample at t=0, mirroring the convention used by sibling observed-only ESM files (ope.esm, oh_production.esm, gwp.esm).\n\nEquation cascade: the six equations form a topologically-ordered cascade — L_OH, L_HO2, L_HOx, HO2_ss and P_O3 depend only on inputs and parameters; chain_length depends on the output L_HOx. chain_length's equation is written `chain_length ~ (k_HO2_NO·HO2·NO) / L_HOx` (referencing the L_HOx output variable) rather than fully inlined — this preserves the upstream symbolic structure (S&P writes the HOx chain length as a propagation rate divided by a termination rate) instead of the fully-expanded form, mirroring the ope.esm / oh_production.esm cascade convention. Equation order in the `equations` array follows the upstream `eqs` array verbatim (L_OH, L_HO2, L_HOx, HO2_ss, P_O3, chain_length); since L_HOx precedes chain_length the order is already a valid topological sort.\n\nNumerical defaults: the upstream input @variables carry no defaults — the .esm defaults are taken from the canonical upstream test scenario shared by the four `COOxidation` equation-verification tests (`Eq 6.18 HO2 Steady-State`, `Net O3 Production Rate`, `HOx Loss Rate`, `Chain Length`, test/test_co_oxidation.jl:60-192), all in SI m⁻³:\n  CO  = 2.5e18 m⁻³  (~100 ppb at STP air number density 2.5e25 m⁻³)\n  OH  = 1.0e12 m⁻³  (~1e6 molec/cm³, typical daytime tropospheric OH)\n  HO₂ = 1.0e14 m⁻³  (~1e8 molec/cm³, typical daytime tropospheric HO₂)\n  NO  = 2.5e15 m⁻³  (~0.1 ppb at STP)\n  NO₂ = 2.5e16 m⁻³  (~1 ppb at STP)\n  O₃  = 1.0e18 m⁻³  (~40 ppb at STP, typical tropospheric ozone)\nAt these defaults: L_OH = 9.23e11, L_HO2 = 2.283e12, L_HOx = 3.08e11, P_O3 = 1.752e12 m⁻³·s⁻¹, HO2_ss = 2.963e13 m⁻³, chain_length = 6.575 (dimensionless).\n\nUpstream test reproduction:\n- `COOxidation: Structural Verification` (test_co_oxidation.jl:4-32) — reproduced by the .esm structural shape (12 @variable entries = 6 parameter + 6 state, 6 equations, 7 parameter entries).\n- `COOxidation: Rate Constants at 298 K` (test_co_oxidation.jl:197-211, asserts the six k_* defaults) — reproduced implicitly by the declared rate-constant defaults; `upstream_defaults_all_outputs` pins all six outputs, each computed from the rate constants, so any wrong default shifts a pinned value.\n- `COOxidation: Eq 6.18 HO2 Steady-State (High NOx)` (test_co_oxidation.jl:60-93, upstream rtol=0.05 on HO2_ss≈2.963e13) — reproduced and tightened by `upstream_defaults_all_outputs` at rel=1e-10.\n- `COOxidation: Net O3 Production Rate` (test_co_oxidation.jl:95-126, upstream rtol=0.01 on P_O3≈1.752e12) — reproduced and tightened by `upstream_defaults_all_outputs`.\n- `COOxidation: HOx Loss Rate` (test_co_oxidation.jl:128-158, upstream rtol=0.01 on L_HOx≈3.08e11) — reproduced and tightened by `upstream_defaults_all_outputs`.\n- `COOxidation: Chain Length` (test_co_oxidation.jl:160-192, upstream rtol=0.02 on chain_length≈6.58, bounded 1 < chain_length < 100) — reproduced and tightened by `upstream_defaults_all_outputs`.\n- `COOxidation: HOx Catalytic Cycling` (test_co_oxidation.jl:368-395, asserts chain_length > 1 and P_O3 > 0 at defaults) — reproduced by `upstream_defaults_all_outputs` (pins chain_length=6.575 > 1 and P_O3=1.752e12 > 0).\n- `COOxidation: High NOx - Short Chain Length` (test_co_oxidation.jl:272-303, asserts L_HOx(high NOx) > L_HOx(low NOx) and chain_length > 0 for both) — reproduced by the pair `upstream_high_nox_short_chain_low_nox` / `upstream_high_nox_short_chain_high_nox`, which pin both endpoints at rel=1e-10 (L_HOx 1.395e11 < 3.7645e12; both chain lengths positive — the upstream cross-test inequality becomes a structural fact under the two pinned values).\n- `COOxidation: Qualitative - Positive Production and Loss` (test_co_oxidation.jl:337-366, sweeps OH×HO₂×NO over a 3×3×3 grid and asserts L_OH/L_HO2/L_HOx/HO2_ss all > 0) — reproduced by `upstream_qualitative_low_corner` and `upstream_qualitative_high_corner`, the two extreme corners of that grid, which pin all four (positive) loss/steady-state outputs at rel=1e-10.\nThe remaining tests extend coverage beyond the upstream suite: term isolation by zeroing O₃ / CO / HO₂, single-input doubling of OH / NO / NO₂ / CO / O₃, the low-NOx long-chain and high-NOx short-chain regimes, the HO₂-self-reaction-dominated termination regime, and a `two`-constant override sentinel.\n\nTolerance rationale (model-level rel=1.0e-10 / abs=1.0e-12): the six algebraic equations are small expression trees — the largest (L_OH, L_HO2) sum three 3-factor products (≤11 IEEE-754 ops); HO2_ss and chain_length each add one division; P_O3 subtracts two products from a third. Each output is correctly-rounded to within a few ULP ≈ 1e-15 relative; rel=1e-10 leaves ~5 orders of magnitude of headroom and is far tighter than the upstream rtol=0.01-0.05. The abs=1.0e-12 floor gates the exact-IEEE-754-zero outputs that arise at the term-isolation boundaries (HO2_ss=0 when CO=0; L_HO2=0 and chain_length=0 when HO₂=0), catching the stuck-at-non-zero failure mode. Matches sibling observed-only ESM tolerance: ope.esm rel=1e-10/abs=1e-12, oh_production.esm rel=1e-10/abs=1e-12.\n\nUnit balance (verified): all six equations balance dimensionally.\n  L_OH  `k_CO_OH·CO·OH + k_OH_NO2·OH·NO2 + k_OH_O3·OH·O3`: each term (m³/s)·(m⁻³)·(m⁻³) = m⁻³·s⁻¹ ✓\n  L_HO2 `k_HO2_NO·HO2·NO + two·k_HO2_HO2·HO2² + k_HO2_O3·HO2·O3`: (m³/s)·(m⁻³)·(m⁻³) and (1)·(m³/s)·(m⁻⁶) = m⁻³·s⁻¹ ✓\n  L_HOx `k_OH_NO2·OH·NO2 + two·k_HO2_HO2·HO2²`: (m³/s)·(m⁻³)·(m⁻³) and (1)·(m³/s)·(m⁻⁶) = m⁻³·s⁻¹ ✓\n  HO2_ss `k_CO_OH·CO·OH / (k_HO2_NO·NO)`: (m⁻³·s⁻¹)/(s⁻¹) = m⁻³ ✓\n  P_O3  `k_HO2_NO·HO2·NO − k_OH_O3·OH·O3 − k_HO2_O3·HO2·O3`: each term (m³/s)·(m⁻³)·(m⁻³) = m⁻³·s⁻¹ ✓\n  chain_length `k_HO2_NO·HO2·NO / L_HOx`: (m⁻³·s⁻¹)/(m⁻³·s⁻¹) = dimensionless ✓\nAll concentrations in SI m⁻³, rate constants in SI m³/s, production/loss rates in m⁻³·s⁻¹, chain length dimensionless. SI throughout; no unit conversion at parse or runtime."
            },
            "tolerance": {
                "rel": 1e-10,
                "abs": 1e-12
            },
            "variables": {
                "two": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "1",
                    "default": 2.0,
                    "description": "Stoichiometric factor for the HO₂ self-reaction HO₂ + HO₂ → H₂O₂ + O₂ (S&P §6.3 R6.15): two HO₂ radicals are consumed per reaction event, so the HOx loss contributed by the self-reaction is two·k_HO2_HO2·[HO₂]². Upstream this is an MTK `@constants` entry (default 2, dimensionless); it is preserved here as `type: parameter` with a fixed default of 2.0 so MTK's unit checker sees a named dimensionless quantity rather than a magic literal in the L_HO2 and L_HOx right-hand sides, and so downstream consumers can override the stoichiometric factor for sensitivity studies. The `two_constant_override_sentinel` test overrides it to 1.0 to catch a regression that hard-codes the factor as a literal."
                },
                "k_CO_OH": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^3/s",
                    "default": 2.4e-19,
                    "description": "CO + OH → CO₂ + H rate constant (S&P §6.3 R6.9, Table B.1, 298 K). The upstream default 2.4e-19 m³/s is the SI-converted form of the CGS value 2.4e-13 cm³/molec/s. This is the chain-initiating step of the CO-oxidation cycle — it consumes CO and an OH radical, producing the H atom that promptly forms HO₂. k_CO_OH appears in the OH loss rate L_OH and (via the CO·OH product) in the high-NOx HO₂ steady state HO2_ss. The clean literal 2.4e-19 differs from the upstream Julia product 2.4e-13·1.0e-6 = 2.3999999999999997e-19 by exactly one ULP (see model reference notes)."
                },
                "k_HO2_NO": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^3/s",
                    "default": 8.1e-18,
                    "description": "HO₂ + NO → OH + NO₂ rate constant (S&P §6.3 R6.11, Table B.1, 298 K). The upstream default 8.1e-18 m³/s is the SI-converted form of the CGS value 8.1e-12 cm³/molec/s. This is the O₃-producing chain-propagation step: it regenerates OH (sustaining the catalytic HOx cycle) and converts NO to NO₂, whose subsequent photolysis yields an O₃ molecule. k_HO2_NO appears in the HO₂ loss rate L_HO2, the gross O₃-production term of P_O3, the HO₂ steady-state denominator HO2_ss, and the chain_length numerator. The clean literal 8.1e-18 is bit-identical to the upstream Julia product 8.1e-12·1.0e-6."
                },
                "k_HO2_HO2": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^3/s",
                    "default": 2.9e-18,
                    "description": "HO₂ + HO₂ → H₂O₂ + O₂ rate constant (S&P §6.3 R6.15, Table B.1, 298 K). The upstream default 2.9e-18 m³/s is the SI-converted form of the CGS value 2.9e-12 cm³/molec/s. This is the dominant HOx self-termination channel at low NOx — the HO₂ self-reaction that converts two HO₂ radicals into stable hydrogen peroxide. It enters the HO₂ loss rate L_HO2 and the HOx loss rate L_HOx as the quadratic term two·k_HO2_HO2·[HO₂]² (the factor `two` for the two-HO₂-per-event stoichiometry). The clean literal 2.9e-18 is bit-identical to the upstream Julia product 2.9e-12·1.0e-6."
                },
                "k_OH_NO2": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^3/s",
                    "default": 1e-17,
                    "description": "OH + NO₂ + M → HNO₃ rate constant (S&P §6.3 R6.12, Table B.1, 298 K). The upstream default 1.0e-17 m³/s is the SI-converted form of the CGS value 1.0e-11 cm³/molec/s. This termolecular reaction is the dominant daytime HOx/NOx termination at high NOx: it removes an OH radical and an NO₂ molecule, converting reactive nitrogen into nitric acid. It enters the OH loss rate L_OH and the HOx loss rate L_HOx. The clean literal 1.0e-17 differs from the upstream Julia product 1.0e-11·1.0e-6 = 9.999999999999999e-18 by exactly one ULP (see model reference notes)."
                },
                "k_HO2_O3": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^3/s",
                    "default": 2e-21,
                    "description": "HO₂ + O₃ → OH + 2O₂ rate constant (S&P §6.3, Table B.1, 298 K). The upstream default 2.0e-21 m³/s is the SI-converted form of the CGS value 2.0e-15 cm³/molec/s. This is a slow ozone-loss channel — HO₂ reacting directly with ozone — that subtracts from net O₃ production P_O3 and adds to the HO₂ loss rate L_HO2. The clean literal 2.0e-21 differs from the upstream Julia product 2.0e-15·1.0e-6 = 2.0000000000000002e-21 by exactly one ULP (see model reference notes)."
                },
                "k_OH_O3": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^3/s",
                    "default": 7.3e-20,
                    "description": "OH + O₃ → HO₂ + O₂ rate constant (S&P §6.3, Table B.1, 298 K). The upstream default 7.3e-20 m³/s is the SI-converted form of the CGS value 7.3e-14 cm³/molec/s. This ozone-loss channel — OH reacting with ozone — subtracts from net O₃ production P_O3 and adds to the OH loss rate L_OH. The clean literal 7.3e-20 is bit-identical to the upstream Julia product 7.3e-14·1.0e-6."
                },
                "CO": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^-3",
                    "default": 2.5e+18,
                    "description": "Carbon monoxide number density [m⁻³] (externally-supplied input). The default 2.5e18 m⁻³ ≈ 100 ppb at STP air number density (2.5e25 m⁻³) and matches the canonical upstream test scenario (test_co_oxidation.jl:65). CO is the oxidized substrate of the cycle — it appears in the OH loss term k_CO_OH·CO·OH of L_OH and (via the CO·OH product) in the HO₂ steady state HO2_ss. Encoded as `type: parameter` because COOxidation has no governing equation for CO — it is purely an input, set via `parameter_overrides` in tests and `parameter_sweep` in examples."
                },
                "OH": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^-3",
                    "default": 1000000000000.0,
                    "description": "Hydroxyl radical number density [m⁻³] (externally-supplied input from the coupled HOx chemistry). The default 1.0e12 m⁻³ ≈ 1e6 molec/cm³ is the typical daytime tropospheric OH concentration and matches the canonical upstream test scenario. OH appears in all three terms of the OH loss rate L_OH, in the OH+NO₂ term of the HOx loss rate L_HOx, in the OH+O₃ loss term of P_O3, and in the HO₂ steady state HO2_ss. Encoded as `type: parameter` (externally-supplied input with no governing equation in this component)."
                },
                "HO2": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^-3",
                    "default": 100000000000000.0,
                    "description": "Hydroperoxyl radical number density [m⁻³] (externally-supplied input). The default 1.0e14 m⁻³ ≈ 1e8 molec/cm³ is the typical daytime tropospheric HO₂ concentration and matches the canonical upstream test scenario. HO₂ appears in all three terms of the HO₂ loss rate L_HO2 (linearly in the HO₂+NO and HO₂+O₃ terms, quadratically in the self-reaction term), in the quadratic self-reaction term of L_HOx, and linearly in the gross-production and HO₂+O₃-loss terms of P_O3 and in the chain_length numerator. Encoded as `type: parameter`."
                },
                "NO": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^-3",
                    "default": 2500000000000000.0,
                    "description": "Nitric oxide number density [m⁻³] (externally-supplied input). The default 2.5e15 m⁻³ ≈ 0.1 ppb at STP and matches the canonical upstream test scenario. NO drives the O₃-producing HO₂+NO chain-propagation step: it appears in the HO₂+NO term of L_HO2, the gross-production term of P_O3, the chain_length numerator, and (in the denominator) the HO₂ steady state HO2_ss. Encoded as `type: parameter`. Note: HO2_ss = k_CO_OH·CO·OH / (k_HO2_NO·NO) divides by NO, so NO must remain strictly positive — the inline tests keep NO ≥ 1e14 m⁻³."
                },
                "NO2": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^-3",
                    "default": 2.5e+16,
                    "description": "Nitrogen dioxide number density [m⁻³] (externally-supplied input). The default 2.5e16 m⁻³ ≈ 1 ppb at STP and matches the canonical upstream test scenario (NO/NO₂ ratio 0.1, the canonical daytime photostationary partitioning). NO₂ appears in the OH+NO₂ termination term of both the OH loss rate L_OH and the HOx loss rate L_HOx — so raising NO₂ shortens the HOx chain. Encoded as `type: parameter`."
                },
                "O3": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^-3",
                    "default": 1e+18,
                    "description": "Ozone number density [m⁻³] (externally-supplied input). The default 1.0e18 m⁻³ ≈ 40 ppb at STP is a typical tropospheric ozone concentration and matches the canonical upstream test scenario. O₃ appears in the OH+O₃ loss term of L_OH, the HO₂+O₃ loss term of L_HO2, and both ozone-loss terms subtracted in the net O₃ production rate P_O3. Encoded as `type: parameter`."
                },
                "P_O3": {
                    "type": "unknown",
                    "units": "m^-3*s^-1",
                    "description": "Net ozone production rate [m⁻³·s⁻¹] (S&P §6.3, Eqs. 6.9-6.13): P_O3 = k_HO2_NO·HO₂·NO − k_OH_O3·OH·O₃ − k_HO2_O3·HO₂·O₃. The gross production term k_HO2_NO·HO₂·NO counts the O₃ molecules made via HO₂ + NO → OH + NO₂ followed by NO₂ photolysis; the two subtracted terms are the OH+O₃ and HO₂+O₃ ozone-loss channels. At the default scenario P_O3 ≈ 1.752e12 m⁻³·s⁻¹ (net positive — production dominates). P_O3 can go negative when NO is too low to drive much production while O₃ is high (see `upstream_qualitative_low_corner`) or when HO₂ vanishes so only the OH+O₃ loss term survives (see `zero_ho2_net_o3_destruction`)."
                },
                "L_HOx": {
                    "type": "unknown",
                    "units": "m^-3*s^-1",
                    "description": "HOx radical-termination loss rate [m⁻³·s⁻¹] (S&P §6.3, Eqs. 6.11-6.12): L_HOx = k_OH_NO2·OH·NO₂ + two·k_HO2_HO2·HO₂². The two radical-termination channels — OH + NO₂ + M → HNO₃ (the high-NOx NHL channel) and the HO₂ + HO₂ → H₂O₂ self-reaction (the low-NOx HHL channel, carrying the stoichiometric factor `two`). At the default scenario L_HOx ≈ 3.08e11 m⁻³·s⁻¹. L_HOx is the denominator of the HOx chain length: a smaller L_HOx (low NOx, low HO₂) gives a longer catalytic chain."
                },
                "L_OH": {
                    "type": "unknown",
                    "units": "m^-3*s^-1",
                    "description": "OH radical loss rate [m⁻³·s⁻¹] (S&P §6.3, Eq. 6.17 denominator): L_OH = k_CO_OH·CO·OH + k_OH_NO2·OH·NO₂ + k_OH_O3·OH·O₃ — the total rate at which OH is consumed by reaction with CO, with NO₂, and with O₃. At the default scenario L_OH ≈ 9.23e11 m⁻³·s⁻¹. Linear in OH; the OH+CO channel dominates at the default CO=2.5e18 (~100 ppb) loading."
                },
                "L_HO2": {
                    "type": "unknown",
                    "units": "m^-3*s^-1",
                    "description": "HO₂ radical loss rate [m⁻³·s⁻¹] (S&P §6.3): L_HO2 = k_HO2_NO·HO₂·NO + two·k_HO2_HO2·HO₂² + k_HO2_O3·HO₂·O₃ — the total rate at which HO₂ is consumed by reaction with NO, by the HO₂ self-reaction (with the stoichiometric factor `two`), and by reaction with O₃. At the default scenario L_HO2 ≈ 2.283e12 m⁻³·s⁻¹, dominated by the HO₂+NO chain-propagation channel. Quadratic in HO₂ through the self-reaction term, linear in HO₂ through the other two."
                },
                "chain_length": {
                    "type": "unknown",
                    "units": "1",
                    "description": "HOx chain length [dimensionless] (S&P §6.3): chain_length = (k_HO2_NO·HO₂·NO) / L_HOx — the propagation rate of the O₃-producing HO₂ + NO step divided by the HOx termination rate, i.e. the number of OH-HO₂ catalytic cycles a HOx radical completes before it is terminated. The equation references the L_HOx output variable rather than inlining its expression, preserving the upstream symbolic structure. At the default scenario chain_length ≈ 6.575 (a HOx radical drives ~6-7 O₃-producing cycles before termination). chain_length > 1 confirms genuine catalytic cycling; it rises in the low-NOx long-chain regime and falls below 1 in the high-NOx short-chain regime."
                },
                "HO2_ss": {
                    "type": "unknown",
                    "units": "m^-3",
                    "description": "HO₂ steady-state concentration in the high-NOx limit [m⁻³] (S&P §6.3 Eq. 6.18): HO2_ss = k_CO_OH·CO·OH / (k_HO2_NO·NO). At high NOx, HO₂ is removed primarily by reaction with NO, so the steady-state HO₂ balances its production from the CO + OH → ... → HO₂ initiation against the HO₂ + NO loss. At the default scenario HO2_ss ≈ 2.963e13 m⁻³. Proportional to CO and OH, inversely proportional to NO; it is a diagnostic estimate independent of the actual HO₂ input used elsewhere in the component."
                }
            },
